      "dom_id": "question-79836",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Estimate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)=f(1,0.12),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1,0.12).$\n\nMoving from $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)$ to $(1,0.12),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}, \\\\ Δy & =0.12-0=0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0.12) \\\\  & ≈0+0.5\\cdot 0.12 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.06.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0.12) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)+0.06 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12) & ≈\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Incorrect",
